Evidence classes used throughout: [O] observed fact in the data, [M] model-derived relationship, [S] statistical association, [E] expert judgement, [C] causal claim. The MVP should make almost no [C] claims.

1. Executive verdict

Position B: retain Failure Pattern Intelligence as the MVP, and redefine the long-term capability.

The original proposal survives, but five things change: the central question, the prioritisation logic, the evidence hierarchy, the control-rating claim and the removal of people analysis.

I reject C (expanding now to “Operational Failure and Resilience Intelligence”) for four reasons:

The structural data is the weakest data. Product is not structured, process-to-application links are human-made, and Holocentric content describes the designed process, not the lived one.
A Product × Process × Application × Business Unit × Control Environment cell is too sparse. Spread 139,744 incidents across thousands of such cells and most cells hold a handful of events. Findings at that grain cannot be validated.
The validation burden multiplies. Pattern validity, structural-link validity and remediation effect would all need proving at once, and a failure in any one would be hard to attribute.
The benefit of C is real but sequential. Structure explains patterns only after you have trustworthy patterns to explain.

On Challenge 3, the useful distinction is this: the failure pattern is the outcome unit, and the operating model is the explanatory structure. Process, application and product are not mere metadata. They are what lets you ask why a pattern exists and where it might appear next. But they are explanatory variables to be tested, not the thing being counted.

2. What the original proposal got right

Preserve these:

Starting from outcomes, and treating controls as hypotheses to test.
Building patterns from text, so the method tolerates missing or wrong human links.
Treating cross-references as weighted evidence.
Normalising for exposure and reporting behaviour.
Building a human-labelled validation set before anything else, with a cheap early stop if experts cannot agree on what counts as the same mechanism.
Refusing to build a digital twin or prediction layer first.
An evidence trail from every claim back to source records.
3. What the original proposal got wrong or underweighted
“Incidents record what actually happened.” Overstated. They record what was detected, met a threshold and was written down.
Recurrence as the headline. It favours frequent, minor, well-reported events and makes a single novel Severe event invisible.
Process, application and product as attributes only. I asked no structural questions of them.
Remediation effectiveness by owner and business unit. I should not have proposed it. Differences there mostly reflect portfolio, exposure, legacy platforms and reporting culture.
“Phantom controls.” Co-occurrence of an Effective rating and a later failure is not a contradiction until timing, scope and relevance line up.
“Fixes that never fixed.” That is causal wording the data cannot support.
The action-type insight. My claim that “training and awareness” shows the weakest effect is confounded. Lower-severity issues are probably more likely to receive training actions, so the comparison is not like for like.
The pattern definition. One paragraph for the central object of the capability was not enough.
No decision loop. I listed insights without saying who would do what because of them.
Methodology step 7. I called it “the first point at which a causal claim is arguable”. That is too generous, even with comparison groups.
4. Revised central question

Where do our operating arrangements produce, or leave us exposed to, material adverse outcomes, and is what we have done about it changing that?

“Are we paying for the same failures repeatedly?” stays as one sub-question, not the headline.

5. Revised capability

Name: Failure Pattern Intelligence (materiality-led, with structural context). The long-term destination is Operational Failure and Resilience Intelligence (section 11).

Objective: Maintain a validated, versioned catalogue of how the bank’s operating arrangements fail, rank it by consequence, show where each pattern sits in the operating model, and give decision-makers evidence-graded prompts for specific decisions.

Primary unit of analysis: the failure pattern.

Supporting units: process (L2/L3, with tasks in a pilot), application, product, change event, service provider (confirm this data exists), control, issue, action.

Target users: CRO and executive risk committee, Non-Financial Risk, Line 2 control and remediation oversight, Internal Audit planning, and process, product and application owners at role level. Not for individual performance assessment.

Principal outputs:

A pattern catalogue in three lanes (below)
Each pattern’s footprint on process, application and product
Remediation review candidates
Control relevance and rating-challenge candidates, in tiers
A small set of structural exposure hypotheses
A decision log
Confidence statements on every output
Pattern definition (Challenge 4)

A failure pattern is a versioned, expert-validated grouping of events sharing:

Mechanism: what went wrong and how.
Locus: process step, application and product, where evidenced.
Enabling conditions: for example a change event, manual hand-off, third party or volume peak.
Consequence profile: the distribution of customer, financial and regulatory impact.
Intervention class: the kind of response that would address it.

It also carries an evidence set with membership weights, a time span, a version, a validation status and a confidence level.

Granularity rule: split a pattern only if the split implies a different intervention or decision-maker. Merge if experts would apply the same fix. Granularity is set by decision usefulness, not by cluster tidiness.
Overlap: soft membership. An event attaches to up to three patterns with weights, and counts are reported on primary assignment and weighted.
Stability: re-form patterns across time slices, embedding models and parameter ranges. Patterns with low membership overlap are marked provisional and excluded from effect analysis.
Usefulness, not just coherence: experts independently name the same intervention class for the pattern, the pattern beats the existing taxonomy at identifying same-mechanism events, and at least one decision-maker can state the decision it would change.
Prioritisation lanes (Challenge 2)

A single score would let frequency dominate, so I would use three lanes ranked on consequence profile, never summed:

Lane 1, material and recurring: exposure-adjusted loss, customer harm and regulatory impact over time.
Lane 2, severe and novel: no recurrence needed. Ranked by severity, then examined for structurally similar configurations elsewhere.
Lane 3, frequent and low impact: reported as aggregate friction cost, with a capped share of executive attention.

Potential impact is not among the listed fields, so it would have to come from risk assessments and be treated as [E].

6. Evidence model
Rank	Source	Appropriate role	Caveat
1	Incidents and impact fields	Observed consequences, the basis for materiality and recurrence [O]. Mechanisms read from text are [M].	Detected and reported only, with reporting bias.
2	Issues and control-gap fields	Formal recognition of weakness, including before any incident. Co-primary for forward-looking evidence [O].	Assessor judgement and rating inconsistency.
3	Near misses	Weak-signal evidence and a reporting-health indicator. “No Impact” incidents may be the best proxy (confirm).	Possibly sparse, inconsistent across units.
4	Actions and closure summaries	What the organisation did [O]. Whether it was implemented, scoped and verified is only readable from text [M].	Closure is activity, not outcome.
5	Control assessments	Time-bound expert judgements [E].	Often stale or unscoped.
6	Control descriptions	Design intent. What the bank expects to happen.	Says nothing about what does happen.
7	Processes and tasks	Designed structure. Explains where patterns sit.	May not match practice, and may be out of date.
8	Applications	Dependencies and concentrations.	Same currency problem.
9	Products	Extracted from text [M]. Pilot on one or two families.	Extraction error.
10	Cross-references	Human assertions, one signal among several. Weight learned from validation, not assumed.	Incomplete and inconsistent.
11	Financial and customer fields	Attributes of outcomes, used for materiality.	Write-offs understate cost, and timing varies.

Is “incident equals reality” too strong? Yes. Incidents are the best record of detected and reported consequences, and the right base for consequence and recurrence. They are not the whole picture.

Avoiding a purely backward-looking capability:

Issues and control-gap indicators carry forward-looking weight.
Lane 2 applies severe patterns to configurations that have not yet failed.
The case in the critique (issue raised, action completed, no incident) is not a blind spot to avoid but a comparison group: similar issues that were remediated without a later incident, set against those followed by incidents. It cannot prove success, because detection and survivorship effects remain, but it gives the remediation analysis something to be compared against.
7. MVP boundary

In:

Incidents, issues, actions, control descriptions and assessments
Process (L2/L3) and application context
Product extraction for one or two families
Three-lane prioritisation
An evidence trail and confidence labels
A decision log

Scope trade-off: the pattern catalogue can be built enterprise-wide, because embedding the text is cheap. Validation and findings release should cover only one or two domains (for example, payments) end to end. Validation effort is the scarce resource.

Out:

Any analysis by owner, individual or team
Prediction, causal modelling or a digital twin
Automated changes to ratings or issues
Task-level process analysis beyond a few pilot processes
Enterprise-wide product taxonomy
Obligation mapping (later phase)
New near-miss data capture
A general chat interface over the data
