If findings do not change decisions, is the capability unsuccessful? For the investment case, largely yes. Two clarifications apply. A documented decision to take no action, with reasons, counts as a decision. And decisions that were considered and rejected are a healthy sign, not a failure.

9. Validation framework
Human-labelled data: a few hundred incident pairs, stratified across close, distant and hard-negative cases, labelled same or different mechanism by at least two experts, with adjudication and agreement reported. Separate sets for link plausibility (incident to control, action to issue root cause), whether an action targets the mechanism, and product extraction. Hold out a time slice.
Retrieval validation: seed known historical repeat failures and measure recall and precision against the existing taxonomy and keyword baselines. If the approach does not clearly beat the baseline, stop here.
Pattern validation: expert review of sampled members, the three usefulness tests above, and stability across embedding models and parameters.
Temporal validation: form patterns on data to year T, then check how later events are assigned and whether patterns survive taxonomy and system changes.
Remediation-effect validation:
Methods: interrupted time series and difference-in-differences against comparable patterns or scopes without an action, with placebo closure dates, negative-control patterns, pre-trend checks and time-to-recurrence analysis.
Denominators: transaction and customer volumes, change and release counts, and reporting-rate indicators such as logging lag and near-miss ratio.
What an action’s closure plus later recurrence supports: “similar events were observed after closure in the same scope, at a rate above/below the exposure-adjusted expectation [S]”. It does not support “the action failed”.
Distinctions: completion is [O]; implementation is [M] or [E] from closure text; quality and scope are [E]; effectiveness is [S]; sustainability is time since closure. These must be reported separately.
Prohibited wording: “failed”, “ineffective”, “proved”, “caused”, “the fix did not work”. Use “recurrence observed after closure”, “reduction consistent with”, or “insufficient evidence”.
False-positive and false-negative review: sample flagged and unflagged cases, and seed known misses from audit findings and regulator observations.
Confidence reporting: every finding states its evidence class, sample size, interval, link tier and an explicit list of unknowns.
Regulatory and model-risk review: register it under model risk policy with an intended-use statement, independent validation and documented limits. Include a privacy review, since customer-harm text may contain personal information, and confirm text is processed in an approved environment. It is also worth mapping outputs to prudential operational-risk expectations (CPS 230) so supervisors can see how evidence is graded.
Control rating contradiction (Challenge 8)

Three tiers, each requiring more:

Co-occurrence: a control and a failure appear together. No claim.
Candidate misalignment: the control existed before and during the failure and its scope overlaps the process, application or product.
Assurance-rating contradiction: all of the following are confirmed by an expert: the control was active and in scope at the time, its objective was relevant to the mechanism (prevent, detect, mitigate or recover), the failure fell inside its intended boundary, and a valid assessment covering that period exists, with the dimension identified (design or operating; Line 1 or Line 2; overall).

Stale and missing assessments are shown as such, not treated as Effective. For uncertainty, each condition is marked confirmed, probable, unknown or conflicting. The output is the tier plus the list of unknowns, not a single probability.

10. Measurement framework

Success is measured without claiming that the capability caused better outcomes.

Analytical quality: expert agreement, precision and recall against baseline, pattern stability, calibration of stated confidence, and the share of headline findings surviving expert review.
Adoption: repeat use by named decision-makers in actual forums. Logins do not count.
Decisions changed: the decision log, with decision yield per finding, including rejections and documented no-action decisions.
Financial and customer outcomes: exposure-adjusted loss and harm within affected patterns, tracked against comparison areas over 12 to 24 months and labelled as association [S].
Remediation review outcomes: the share of reviews that find real gaps, which is also the precision of the flag, against review cost.
Control-assurance outcomes: the share of challenges that reach a changed rating or design fix, and the share of candidates confirmed.
Reporting-behaviour safeguards:
Track logging rates, logging lag and near-miss ratios by area before and after release, and treat a fall as a warning.
Report an increase in logging as a positive signal, not a deterioration.
Stage the rollout across domains. That gives a comparison without a formal experiment.
Accountability and guardrails (Challenge 7)
No owner, individual or team ranking. Accountability sits at the level of process, product or application role and the enterprise forum.
Minimum cell sizes, so small groups cannot be identified.
A written prohibition on use in performance management or discipline.
A no-blame statement that reporting and issue-raising are valued.
Access controls and periodic review of how outputs are used.
11. Next-level capability

If the MVP works: Structural Fragility and Transfer Intelligence.

Structural fragility: which operating-model characteristics associate with pattern frequency and severity [S]. Examples are manual hand-offs, number of applications touched, shared application dependencies, service-provider concentration, and change events.
Transfer: apply a validated pattern to configurations that have not failed, for example processes sharing an application, hand-off structure or provider. Output is a ranked set of exposure hypotheses for expert review, labelled [M] and [E].
Precursors and severity migration: lead-time analysis from low-rated events to severe ones.
Change-linked risk: relating change events to later pattern emergence.
Stage	What it offers	Gate
Description	Pattern catalogue	Patterns pass validation
Diagnosis	Footprint and structural association	Structural links validated on pilot processes
Decision support	Review and escalation prompts	Decision log shows use
Early warning	Precursor alerts	Out-of-time skill beats base rate, with an acceptable false-alarm cost
Prediction	Forecasts for named pattern classes	Out-of-time forecasts beat simple baselines over several cycles, with exposure data available
Causal model	Estimated intervention effects	Quasi-experimental evidence across many patterns, expert-validated mechanisms
Digital twin	Simulate changes	Dependency data maintained as a living asset, and the model reproduces held-out history and counterfactual changes

A digital twin is justified only once all earlier gates have been passed. For this bank I would expect that to take years, not quarters, mainly because the structure data would need to be maintained automatically.

12. Final recommendation
Fund: the Failure Pattern Intelligence MVP, materiality-led, with structural context and a decision log.
Do not fund yet: the predictive platform and operational digital twin. Also hold enterprise-wide task-level process mining until the pilot shows the structural data is good enough.
“Wow” insight to target: the number of closed, high-rated issues whose closure narrative addresses a different mechanism or narrower scope than the pattern that later appeared in the same process scope, confirmed by experts. It is model-derived, it points to a specific decision (reopen or review), and it does not claim the remediation failed.
Major risk that could invalidate the proposition: pattern validity. If experts cannot reliably agree on what counts as the same mechanism, or the patterns do not beat the existing taxonomy and do not point to distinct interventions, nothing built on them is sound. This is why the validation set and the baseline comparison come first, and why the first stop-or-go decision falls early and cheaply.
